WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.979
Satellite radar sees the ocean day and night,
through any cloud cover,

00:00:05.059 --> 00:00:07.650
capturing wind streaks, convective cells,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:11.036
and other atmospheric signatures on the sea surface.

00:00:11.116 --> 00:00:16.573
But teaching machines to read these images is hard,
because expert labels are scarce.

00:00:16.653 --> 00:00:22.109
This work asks whether self-supervised learning on
three million unlabeled Sentinel-1

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:25.430
radar images can overcome that labeling bottleneck,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:27.710
and honestly reports what it found.

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:34.699
The European Space Agency's Sentinel-1 radar
satellites image the global ocean at unprecedented scale,

00:00:34.779 --> 00:00:38.568
capturing waves, turbulence, fronts, and biological
slicks.

00:00:38.648 --> 00:00:41.257
Computer vision can process these images,

00:00:41.337 --> 00:00:45.585
but machine learning has been held back by a lack
of labeled data,

00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:49.257
since only trained experts can annotate radar vignettes.

00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:56.799
Earlier work also relied on a biased dataset that
picked only exemplary images and forced a single label per image,

00:00:56.879 --> 00:00:59.881
even though multiple phenomena usually coexist.

00:00:59.961 --> 00:01:05.980
The result was a training set that did not reflect
the real distribution of ocean conditions.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.194
Contrastive self-supervised learning has
transformed computer vision by training networks

00:01:10.274 --> 00:01:14.109
on huge pools of unlabeled data to produce
embeddings that transfer well to downstream

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:18.023
tasks.

00:01:18.103 --> 00:01:21.938
It has worked for natural images and medical images,

00:01:22.018 --> 00:01:25.852
but has rarely been applied to remote sensing.

00:01:25.932 --> 00:01:29.767
That gap matters here,

00:01:29.847 --> 00:01:33.681
because Sentinel-1 alone collects roughly a hundred
and twenty thousand Wave-mode ocean images every single month.

00:01:33.761 --> 00:01:37.596
This study tests the hypothesis that contrastive
learning on that unlabeled stream can

00:01:37.676 --> 00:01:41.510
overcome the scarcity of expert labels for SAR analysis.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.717
This paper makes three contributions.

00:01:45.797 --> 00:01:49.624
First,

00:01:49.704 --> 00:01:53.531
it builds a new hand-labeled dataset of about
twenty-three hundred randomly sampled,

00:01:53.611 --> 00:01:57.438
multi-label radar observations that better
represents the real ocean population.

00:01:57.518 --> 00:02:01.345
Second, it leverages three years of unlabeled
Sentinel-1 imagery,

00:02:01.425 --> 00:02:05.252
roughly three million images, to train a SwAV
contrastive embedding of SAR scenes.

00:02:05.332 --> 00:02:09.159
Third,

00:02:09.239 --> 00:02:13.066
it rigorously compares that self-supervised
representation against standard transfer learning

00:02:13.146 --> 00:02:16.973
from ImageNet and against the previous
state-of-the-art CmWV classifier,

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:20.880
using three different downstream evaluation protocols.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.796
The core method is SwAV,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:28.519
a contrastive framework that assigns image
representations to clusters and trains the

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:33.704
network to predict the cluster assignment of one
augmented view from another.

00:02:33.784 --> 00:02:35.724
Unlike methods such as SimCLR,

00:02:35.804 --> 00:02:41.716
SwAV avoids prohibitively large batches by storing
recent cluster assignments in a queue.

00:02:41.796 --> 00:02:47.911
The authors train a standard ResNet-50 backbone
with SwAV using a batch size of one thousand

00:02:47.991 --> 00:02:50.267
twenty-four across eight V100 GPUs,

00:02:50.347 --> 00:02:54.509
a queue of sixteen batches, and one thousand
cluster centroids,

00:02:54.589 --> 00:02:58.751
stopping after sixty-five epochs and about ten days
of compute.

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:01.040
To measure representation quality,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.292
they use three downstream protocols: a weighted
k-nearest-neighbor classifier,

00:03:06.372 --> 00:03:11.679
a linear evaluation that freezes the backbone and
trains a single softmax layer,

00:03:11.759 --> 00:03:16.190
and full end-to-end fine-tuning of all weights on
the labeled data.

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.470
Two datasets drive the study.

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:26.252
The pretraining pool is nearly three million
unlabeled Wave-mode images from Sentinel-1 A and B over 2017 to 2019,

00:03:26.332 --> 00:03:31.918
each covering a twenty by twenty kilometer patch of
ocean at five meter resolution,

00:03:31.998 --> 00:03:36.902
ninety percent used for training and ten percent
held out for validation.

00:03:36.982 --> 00:03:42.841
The labeled benchmark contains twenty-three hundred
vignettes annotated by experts into

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:48.643
a consensus multi-label ground truth across four
classes: mesoscale convection cells,

00:03:48.723 --> 00:03:51.920
wind streaks, negligible variability, and other.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.450
This set is stratified and split sixty, twenty,
twenty into training, validation,

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.020
and held-out test data.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.507
The headline result is that fine-tuned models reach
a best micro-averaged area under the

00:04:03.587 --> 00:04:07.693
ROC curve of about zero point nine three.

00:04:07.773 --> 00:04:11.880
Both the contrastive and the ImageNet-pretrained
models comfortably beat the earlier CmWV

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:16.067
classifier on the two hardest atmospheric classes.

00:04:16.147 --> 00:04:20.253
For wind streaks,

00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:24.440
the new models score in the mid eighties compared
with CmWV's zero point seven three,

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:28.627
and for convection cells they reach about zero
point eight seven versus zero point seven nine.

00:04:28.707 --> 00:04:32.813
On the negligible-variability class, all three
models perform about equally well,

00:04:32.893 --> 00:04:37.000
near zero point nine five.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.894
The most informative comparison is across the three
evaluation protocols.

00:04:40.974 --> 00:04:44.488
When the backbone is frozen,

00:04:44.568 --> 00:04:48.083
the self-supervised contrastive weights hold a small edge,

00:04:48.163 --> 00:04:51.677
scoring zero point eight six four versus zero point
eight five nine under nearest-neighbor

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:55.271
classification and zero point eight four one versus
zero point eight three six under linear

00:04:55.351 --> 00:04:58.865
evaluation.

00:04:58.945 --> 00:05:02.459
But once all weights are fine-tuned end to end,

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:06.053
that gap not only disappears but slightly reverses,

00:05:06.133 --> 00:05:09.648
with the ImageNet initialization reaching zero
point nine three one against the contrastive

00:05:09.728 --> 00:05:13.242
model's zero point nine two nine.

00:05:13.322 --> 00:05:16.836
In other words,

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:20.430
the benefit of self-supervised pretraining vanishes
once the whole network is allowed to adapt.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.884
A few numbers capture the study.

00:05:22.964 --> 00:05:27.865
Self-supervised training used almost three million
unlabeled radar images.

00:05:27.945 --> 00:05:33.587
The labeled benchmark held twenty-three hundred
expert vignettes across four classes.

00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:39.510
The best fine-tuned model reached a micro-averaged
AUROC of about zero point nine three.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:43.751
And on the toughest classes, wind streaks and
convection cells,

00:05:43.831 --> 00:05:51.357
the new models jumped from CmWV's roughly zero
point seven three and zero point seven nine into the mid eighties,

00:05:51.437 --> 00:05:53.780
a substantial detection improvement.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.000
The honest takeaway is that self-supervised
contrastive learning,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.920
at least in this preliminary study,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.840
offers only marginal gains over simply transferring
features from a model trained on natural images,

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.760
while costing far more compute.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.680
Yet both approaches deliver a dramatic improvement
over the previous state-of-the-art

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:17.600
classifier for reading atmospheric phenomena from
ocean radar.

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.520
The authors argue the idea still holds promise, and
that longer training,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:25.440
better tuning,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.360
and pretext tasks designed for remote sensing
deserve exploration before drawing final conclusions.
